      "id": "lpic2-sieve-header-filing",
      "version": 1,
      "track": "LPIC-2",
      "unit": "linux-sieve-filters",
      "topic": "Sieve exact header filing",
      "title": "File a named list without moving unrelated mail",
      "summary": "Write a mail filter that files one named list and safely keeps other messages.",
      "objective": "File messages with an X-List value exactly equal to staff-updates in News only. Ignore ASCII letter case in field names and values; one matching duplicate field is sufficient. Keep all other messages in INBOX only, including a missing or empty X-List, a different list, and the same words appearing only in Subject. Run the filter against every sample. A correct filter must preserve legitimate unmatched mail and avoid extra mailbox copies. Equivalent supported rules are accepted.",
      "retry_guidance": "Read the failing sample’s Expected and Actual destinations. Distinguish a syntax or unsupported-feature error from a condition or rule-order mistake, then edit and run again.",
      "difficulty": "Intermediate",
      "minutes": 20,
      "steps": [
        "Understand the task",
        "Edit and run the filter",
        "Verify all sample outcomes"
      ],
      "guide": [
        {
          "title": "Run the separate club filter",
          "why": "A message has fields such as Subject. This exercise also supplies X-List, a custom field identifying a list. The filter reads that field and chooses a mailbox. These supplied fields are practice inputs, not proof that a sender is trustworthy.",
          "action": "Select Woodland club in Sample message. Select Fill worked example, then Run filter. Read Actual results for every sample.",
          "commands": []
        },
        {
          "title": "Change the matching value",
          "why": "Sieve is a mail-filter language. require \"fileinto\"; enables filing. if introduces a condition; braces group the action to take when it matches. End each simple action with a semicolon. Double quotes contain literal text; nothing runs in a shell.",
          "action": "In Filter, change woodland to not-woodland in the quoted test value, then select Run filter.",
          "commands": []
        },
        {
          "title": "Observe an unwanted extra copy",
          "why": "header :is compares the entire field value, ignoring ASCII letter case by default. An absent field does not match. fileinto chooses a mailbox and cancels the default INBOX copy. When no filing action matches, Sieve keeps the message in INBOX. An unconditional keep after filing would add another copy.",
          "action": "Fill the worked example again. Add keep; after the closing brace and run it. Remove that final keep; and retry.",
          "commands": []
        }
      ],
      "explanation": "The exact field-value test separates the named list from similar names and unrelated fields. A matching fileinto cancels implicit keep; leaving unmatched mail alone preserves INBOX. No unconditional keep is needed after a successful filing action.",
      "hints": [
        "Compare the required field with the selected sample headers. Subject and X-List are different fields.",
        "Use a whole-value comparison when a similar prefix or suffix must not match.",
        "If Actual results show both News and INBOX, inspect actions after the matching rule. Filing does not stop later statements."
      ],
      "sources": [
        "https://www.lpi.org/our-certifications/exam-201-202-objectives/",
        "https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc5228.html"
      ],
      "simplifications": "Bounded Sieve header/size/filing subset with plain represented ASCII headers and named virtual mailboxes. No real mail delivery, host files, shell, subprocess or network. LF script lines are accepted; message size counts canonical CRLF. No address/envelope parsing, redirect, reject, vacation, procmail, variables, MIME decoding or other extensions. Resource errors are explicit and never earn credit. See SIEVE_SUBSET.md.",
