      "id": "lpic2-sieve-compound-filter",
      "version": 1,
      "track": "LPIC-2",
      "unit": "linux-sieve-filters",
      "topic": "Sieve compound tests and message size",
      "title": "Combine review conditions with a message-size boundary",
      "summary": "Combine project membership, review status and message size without moving unrelated mail.",
      "objective": "File in Review only when X-Project exactly matches atlas and either X-Review exactly matches pending or the complete represented message is larger than 1024 bytes. Ignore ASCII letter case for these exact comparisons. Size includes headers, the blank separator and body, not just body length. Exactly 1024 bytes is not over 1024; pending project mail still qualifies at that boundary. Keep every nonqualifying sample in INBOX only. Run all samples and avoid extra mailbox copies. Equivalent supported conditions are accepted.",
      "retry_guidance": "Read the failing sample’s Expected and Actual destinations. Distinguish a syntax or unsupported-feature error from a condition or rule-order mistake, then edit and run again.",
      "difficulty": "Intermediate",
      "minutes": 24,
      "steps": [
        "Understand the task",
        "Edit and run the filter",
        "Verify all sample outcomes"
      ],
      "guide": [
        {
          "title": "Inspect two different reasons to review",
          "why": "allof (test, test) means every enclosed test must be true. anyof (test, test) means at least one must be true. Separate tests with commas. Parentheses preserve the intended grouping: team membership AND (review needed OR large message).",
          "action": "Fill worked example and select Run filter. Select Small orchard todo, then Large orchard ready. Read their headers, represented size and Actual results.",
          "commands": []
        },
        {
          "title": "Test the exact boundary",
          "why": "size :over compares complete message bytes, including headers and the separating blank line. K means 1024 bytes, so size :over 1K means strictly larger than 1024. At exactly the threshold, neither :over nor :under matches.",
          "action": "Select Ready orchard mail at 512 bytes. Change :over 512 to :over 511 in Filter and run again; restore 512 afterward.",
          "commands": []
        },
        {
          "title": "Compare anyof and allof",
          "why": "A large message from another team must not pass a team-specific rule. Keep the team test outside the anyof group; otherwise size alone could admit unrelated mail. Missing headers do not match, but another true condition inside anyof can still make that group true.",
          "action": "Replace the inner anyof with allof, then run. Compare the small todo and large ready cases. Restore the worked example and rerun.",
          "commands": []
        }
      ],
      "explanation": "The outer allof requires the correct project. Its inner anyof permits pending review or a message over the strict size threshold. Exactly 1024 bytes cannot satisfy the size alternative, but can still qualify through pending status. Other projects remain in INBOX regardless of size.",
      "hints": [
        "Read each failed sample’s project, review field and complete represented size. Separate the outer requirement from the inner alternatives.",
        "The project condition must remain true even when a message is large.",
        "At the size boundary, check whether pending status independently satisfies the alternatives. Do not change :over to an inclusive comparison."
      ],
      "sources": [
        "https://www.lpi.org/our-certifications/exam-201-202-objectives/",
        "https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc5228.html"
      ],
      "simplifications": "Bounded Sieve header/size/filing subset with plain represented ASCII headers and named virtual mailboxes. No real mail delivery, host files, shell, subprocess or network. LF script lines are accepted; message size counts canonical CRLF. No address/envelope parsing, redirect, reject, vacation, procmail, variables, MIME decoding or other extensions. Resource errors are explicit and never earn credit. See SIEVE_SUBSET.md.",
      "initial": {
