      "id": "lpic2-sieve-rule-order",
      "version": 1,
      "track": "LPIC-2",
      "unit": "linux-sieve-filters",
      "topic": "Sieve rule order and safe recovery",
      "title": "Recover an ordered filter without losing unmatched mail",
      "summary": "Repair a filter whose overlapping rules create extra copies and override priority.",
      "objective": "Urgent mail, identified by X-Priority exactly equal to urgent, must go to Review only, even when it also belongs to the staff list. Otherwise, mail with X-List exactly equal to staff-updates must go to News only. Keep all remaining mail in INBOX only. Ignore ASCII letter case and accept one matching duplicate field. Repair the supplied filter and run every sample. Preserve unmatched mail, respect priority and avoid duplicate destinations. Equivalent supported control flow is accepted.",
      "retry_guidance": "Read the failing sample’s Expected and Actual destinations. Distinguish a syntax or unsupported-feature error from a condition or rule-order mistake, then edit and run again.",
      "difficulty": "Intermediate",
      "minutes": 25,
      "steps": [
        "Understand the task",
        "Edit and run the filter",
        "Verify all sample outcomes"
      ],
      "guide": [
        {
          "title": "Diagnose the overlapping starter",
          "why": "Separate if rules are independent. When both match, both bodies run; fileinto does not stop processing. A later keep also adds INBOX, even after another mailbox was chosen.",
          "action": "Run the supplied example before filling it. Select Maintenance notice from the club, then Ordinary club update. Read Actual results.",
          "commands": []
        },
        {
          "title": "Compare a priority repair",
          "why": "An if / elsif / else chain runs only the first matching branch. Alternatively, stop; ends the entire filter at that point. Use these tools to implement priority, rather than relying on the position of a filing action alone. The Stop button cancels the sample run; stop; inside a rule ends processing of that one message.",
          "action": "Select Fill worked example, then Run filter. Inspect the same samples and Unmatched personal mail.",
          "commands": []
        },
        {
          "title": "Try an equivalent control flow",
          "why": "stop without an earlier delivery action still permits implicit keep. discard cancels implicit keep and does not undo other actions, so it is not a repair for an unwanted extra copy. Leave unmatched mail with a deliberate keep or the default INBOX behavior.",
          "action": "Replace the chain with two separate if rules, add stop; after each successful fileinto inside its block, and run.",
          "commands": []
        }
      ],
      "explanation": "The starter runs independent rules and an unconditional keep, so overlapping mail acquires extra destinations. Prioritize urgent mail using an if/elsif chain, or stop after a matching filing action. Preserve the default delivery for everything unmatched.",
      "hints": [
        "Start with the sample matching both urgent and staff-list conditions. Count its actual mailbox destinations.",
        "fileinto does not stop the next independent if, and keep is an additional delivery action.",
        "Either select exactly one branch or explicitly stop after a successful filing. Preserve INBOX for messages matching neither rule."
      ],
      "sources": [
        "https://www.lpi.org/our-certifications/exam-201-202-objectives/",
        "https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc5228.html"
      ],
      "simplifications": "Bounded Sieve header/size/filing subset with plain represented ASCII headers and named virtual mailboxes. No real mail delivery, host files, shell, subprocess or network. LF script lines are accepted; message size counts canonical CRLF. No address/envelope parsing, redirect, reject, vacation, procmail, variables, MIME decoding or other extensions. Resource errors are explicit and never earn credit. See SIEVE_SUBSET.md.",
